# novadeck kernel source pin — Phase 1
# The TARBALL + its sha256 is the canonical pin. Validated 2026-06-17; re-pinned 2026-07-25
# (7.1.5, HW-validated on Pocket S2 + Pocket ACE); re-pinned 2026-08-03 (7.1.6); re-pinned
# 2026-08-18 (7.2 — mainline, released 2026-08-16; NOT HW-validated yet, see the gate below);
# re-pinned 2026-08-27 (7.2.1); re-pinned 2026-09-03 (7.2.3, issue #86).
version:    7.2.3
url:        https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v7.x/linux-7.2.3.tar.xz
sha256:     8ba259e8e7b13ec6ef0941c8a39ad90b24bd4a4d6c0010ba6bafb794550ecd03
git_tag:    v7.2.3
git_commit: 58e7295cfecaddec94629160386412e0f2b1e8fe

# The sha256 above is the one kernel.org publishes in the SIGNED sha256sums.asc for v7.x, not a
# hash of whatever the CDN happened to serve — re-checked 2026-09-03. The .asc itself verifies as
# a GOOD signature from "Kernel.org checksum autosigner <autosigner@kernel.org>", fingerprint
# B886 8C80 BA62 A1FF FAF5 FDA9 632D 3A06 589D A6B1. That key carries no trust path here, so the
# load-bearing check is CONTINUITY: the SAME signed file still lists 7.2.1 as
# 4158098c8a28ea8fbe068c855d3aa6341e03d06282add3cb9049f3a24e4557b3, byte-for-byte the hash this
# pin carried and shipped before — so the file describing 7.2.3 is the same file that described
# the release we already trusted. (Note: keys.openpgp.org serves this key STRIPPED of its user
# IDs and gpg then refuses it as "no public key"; keyserver.ubuntu.com serves it whole.)
# git_commit is the DEREFERENCED tag (`git ls-remote 'v7.2.3^{}'`), matching how the
# 7.1.5/7.1.6/7.2/7.2.1 lines were recorded; the annotated tag object is
# 296e02a60bcb8223c12ebf58feeb69961f4608c1 and is NOT what belongs here.
#
# 7.2 was MAINLINE with no point release; 7.2.1 was the first stable one, and 7.2.3 (2026-09-02)
# is this pin's second in-series step. Precedent from 7.1.3 -> 7.1.5: a point release INSIDE a
# series is a pure pin edit — but that is a prediction, not a licence to skip the patch/config
# gates below. Re-run them.

# Config validation (kernel/*.config) against THIS tree, RE-RUN 2026-09-03 for the 7.2.3 re-pin
# (numbers below are 7.2.3's; the 7.2.1 run they replace found 428/0-missing and 71 patches/26 fuzz,
# and the 7.2 run before that 427/1-missing and 69 patches/21 fuzz):
#   - all 429 referenced CONFIG_* symbols (kernel.config + trim-platforms.config) exist in the
#     PATCHED 7.2.3 tree, with NONE missing. The count moved 428 -> 429 for a reason that has
#     nothing to do with the bump: b4ddf2d added AYANEO_SERIAL_MCU, a symbol our OWN patch
#     1005-add-ayaneo-serial-mcu.patch defines — which is exactly why the sweep must run against
#     the patched tree and not a pristine one. Sweep: strip `CONFIG_x=` and `# CONFIG_x is not set`
#     to bare names, diff against every `config`/`menuconfig` name in the tree's Kconfig files.
#     The one 7.2 casualty stays deleted and its note is still why — see kernel.config's header:
#       READ_ONLY_THP_FOR_FS -> removed upstream in 7.2; file THP is unconditional now.
#     SCHED_CACHE (added 2026-08-22) still resolves the same way (init/Kconfig, `default y`,
#     `depends on SMP`) — the pin records an inherited default rather than changing one.
#   - all 71 out-of-tree patches in kernel/patches/ apply to 7.2.3 with ZERO rejects, applied
#     CUMULATIVELY in lexical order, which is the only order that means anything (see below).
#     26 hunks need fuzz; kernel/build.sh passes no -F0, so patch's default fuzz=2 accepts them.
#     The fuzz count CLIMBS with every point release — when it reaches 2 on a hunk that matters,
#     patch will start applying it in the wrong place silently. So the raw total is the WRONG
#     instrument: 26 was also 7.2.1's total, and a hunk sliding 1 -> 2 while another slid 2 -> 1
#     would leave it unmoved. The 7.2.3 run therefore replayed the identical drill against a
#     pristine 7.2.1 tree and diffed the PER-HUNK profile (patch name + fuzz level, counted): the
#     two are byte-identical, 16 hunks at fuzz 1 and 10 at fuzz 2, same patches on both sides.
#     Nothing climbed.
#   - independent corroboration, and the cheapest signal in this whole drill: applying our patches
#     to BOTH trees and diffing the results isolates the pure upstream delta. 7.2.1 -> 7.2.3 changes
#     82 files; our patches touch 127; the INTERSECTION IS EMPTY. Upstream did not go near a single
#     file we carry a patch for, which is why the fuzz profile could not have moved. Re-run this
#     intersection on the next bump before reading any hunk: a non-empty result names exactly which
#     patches deserve a human, and an empty one retires the question.
#   - `patch unexpectedly ends in middle of line` x4 is NOT a reject: patches 0004 (IFPC) and 0203
#     (S16_LE) have no trailing newline. Both hunks succeed. Pre-existing, unrelated to any bump.
#   - the fragments were then MERGED for real inside build/Dockerfile (`merge_config.sh -m
#     defconfig kernel/*.config` + `make olddefconfig`, trim-platforms.config included) and every
#     requested symbol asserted to have kept its requested value. merge_config emitted ZERO
#     warnings and zero symbols came out wrong — including DEBUG_INFO_BTF/SCHED_CLASS_EXT, which
#     resolve correctly here because the container ships `dwarves` (pahole v1.31).
#
# HOW TO RE-RUN THIS VALIDATION, and the mistake that makes it lie: patches must be applied
# cumulatively to ONE tree, exactly as kernel/build.sh does. Dry-running each patch independently
# against a pristine tree reports ~8 false failures, because several patches depend on files an
# earlier patch CREATES (1002 patches the qcom-hv-haptics.c that 1000 adds; 1003 patches the
# rsinput.c that 0031 adds) and the drm/panel patches all extend one Makefile, each one's
# context containing the line its predecessor added. Zero of those are real.
#
# Corrections applied during the 7.1.5 validation, still current and still load-bearing in
# kernel.config — do not "restore" the left-hand names:
#   CRYPTO_MICHAEL_MIC  -> DROPPED (upstream removed it from crypto/Kconfig; mac80211 carries its
#                          own michael_mic, so TKIP is unaffected). Was already a dead symbol in
#                          7.1.3 and silently ignored by kconfig.
#   MFD_QCOM_SPMI_PMIC  -> MFD_SPMI_PMIC
#   PHY_QCOM_SNPS_EUSB2 -> PHY_SNPS_EUSB2 (+ PHY_QCOM_EUSB2_REPEATER)
#   SM_DISPCC_8650      -> SM_DISPCC_8550 (SM8650 reuses the 8550 display clock controller;
#                          no separate _8650 dispcc upstream — symbols jump 8550 -> 8750)
#
# --- What 7.2 took over from us, and what that cost ---
# Two of our patches went upstream in 7.2 and were DELETED here rather than carried:
#   0101 (sm8550 DDR/LLCC/L3 CPU bandwidth scaling) — landed verbatim; all 11 hunks read as
#         already-applied against 7.2's sm8550.dtsi. Pure deletion, no other change.
#   0065 (sgm3804 panel-power regulator) — landed as a REWRITE, not our version, and with an
#         INCOMPATIBLE DT binding. Upstream's driver registers TWO rails from `pos`/`neg` child
#         nodes each carrying `enable-gpios`; ours was one rail with both GPIOs in `reset-gpios`
#         on the parent, hardcoding 5.2V (reg 0x0c) over I2C while telling the framework 5.0V.
#         Keeping both was not an option: same compatible, same file path, duplicate Kconfig
#         symbol. So the binding was migrated across all six board nodes and the panels that
#         consume it — see the HW gate below, this is the risky half of the bump.
#
# HW GATE, NOT YET RUN. The sgm3804 migration changes how panel power is brought up on
# Pocket ACE / DMG / DS / S1K / S2K, Pocket S2 and konkr-pf:
#   - `avdd-supply` now points at the positive rail only; the negative rail is a SECOND regulator
#     that nothing enabled before, so every panel driver that used to get both rails from one
#     `avdd` now takes an `avee` supply too (patches 0051/0052/0054/0066/0067/0068). A board left
#     un-migrated would come up with AVEE dead — a lit backlight over a black panel.
#   - the rails are pinned to 5200000 uV, NOT the 5000000 the old DTS asked for. 5.2V is what the
#     hardware was actually running: our driver ignored the DT constraint and wrote selector 0x0c,
#     which is 4.0V + 12*100mV on upstream's linear range. Asking for 5.0V here would be a real,
#     silent voltage change dressed up as a no-op.
#   - upstream's driver declares enable_time = 40ms per rail; ours had none. regulator_bulk_enable
#     is async so both rails come up in parallel, but panel prepare is now ~40ms slower.
#
# The migration above was CROSS-CHECKED against the not-yet-merged upstream Pocket S2 display
# series (Neil Armstrong, Linaro), which is the reference for how these nodes are meant to look:
#   [v5] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2: add display nodes -- 2026-07-28, state `new`
#   https://patchwork.kernel.org/project/linux-arm-msm/patch/20260728-topic-sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2-display-dt-v5-1-892ab9bcecfe@linaro.org/
# (lore.kernel.org is behind an Anubis proof-of-work wall and cannot be fetched; patchwork's
# /api/1.2/ and /raw/ endpoints serve the same mail and are not gated. Use those.)
# It independently confirms 5200000 uV on both rails and the same tlmm 59=pos / 58=neg order we
# already had, and it carries three properties this tree was missing, now added to all six nodes:
#   vin-supply = <&vph_pwr>       the chip's input rail (label reachable on every board with an
#                                 sgm3804: qcs8550-ayaneo-pocket-common.dtsi + sm8650-ayaneo-common.dtsi)
#   regulator-active-discharge = <1>   restores the discharge bits our old driver wrote to reg 0x03
#                                 unconditionally; upstream only writes them when DT asks
#   regulator-boot-on             the rail can already be up at handoff (bootloader splash)
#
# WHERE WE STILL DIVERGE, deliberately: upstream names the panel's rails `vsp-supply`/`vsn-supply`
# and drives the S2 panel with a generic `renesas,r63419` driver (compatible "ayaneo,wt0630-2k",
# "renesas,r63419"). Neither that driver nor that series is in 7.2, so our six panel drivers stay
# and keep their own `avdd`/`avee` names. If we ever drop them for the upstream r63419 driver, the
# DTS supply names have to become vsp/vsn at the same time — they are a driver-side contract.
